<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/18th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Campaign</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nader Shah's invasion of India</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1738–1739</span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Indian campaign</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nader-shah">Nader Shah</a>'s invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> was the 1738–1739 campaign in which the founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afsharid-empire">Afsharid Empire</a> crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khyber-pass">Khyber Pass</a>, defeated the Mughal army and sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>. The plunder, including the Mughal imperial treasury, was so vast that Nader cancelled Iranian taxes for three years. The campaign was the high point of Afsharid power and a heavy blow to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nader-shahs-invasion-of-india.jpg" alt="Nader Shah's invasion of India" width="307" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: possibly by Muhammad Ali ibn Abd al-Bayg ibn Ali Quli Jabbadar · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nadir_Shah_at_the_sack_of_Delhi_-_Battle_scene_with_Nader_Shah_on_horseback,_possibly_by_Muhammad_Ali_ibn_Abd_al-Bayg_ibn_Ali_Quli_Jabbadar,_mid-18th_century,_Museum_of_Fine_Arts,_Boston.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
